1. Singleton pattern
We practice the singleton pattern. Suppose you are implementing a scheduler for an operating system. The scheduler is implemented as a singleton class, called ECScheduler. It supports (i) StartTask(int id): start a task with id, (ii) StopTask(int id): stop the task with the id, and (iii) GetNumRunTasks(): get the number of tasks that are running. You can assume the task ids are distinct, and a task runs after starting until it is stopped.
2. Factory method pattern
Let us practice the factory method pattern. Here, we implement part of the maze game class as taught in the video. Briefly, you need the following classes. (i) MazeGame: has a factory method called MakeRoom(); method CreateMaze: create a simple maze with two rooms; GetTotPrize:
get the total prize value for the constructed rooms. (ii) BombedMazeGame: create room with a bomb. (iii) EnchantedMazeGame: create enchanted room. (iv) Room: regular room. Here, each room has a prize value (once visited, you collect this much points); by default, it is 1. There is a GetPrize() method to return the prize. (v) RoomWithBomb: room has a bomb in it. Prize value: -10. (vi) EnchantedRoom. Prize value: 20.
Note: when a maze game is first created, it is empty. You need to call CreateMaze() to populate the rooms.
3. Composite pattern
We first practice the composite pattern. Consider the following situation. An organization (say a university) has different kinds of units. On the low level, there are departments and administrative offices (e.g. Office of Admissions). There are also higher level units, such as school and college. Each unit has a budget to run. Now implement the following classes using the composite pattern. (i) ECDepartment. (ii) ECOffice. (iii) ECCompositeUnit: this is the composite object. Here, we have methods for adding units as its children. (iv) ECUnit: this is the generic interface for all units in the unversity. The main function is GetBudget, which return the entire budget needed to run the unit. The budget of a composite object is simply the sum of the budget of all the lowest level units it contains. Here, we won't create specific composite objects such as school or college since our settings here are simple.
4. Decorator pattern
Decorator pattern is perhaps one of the most useful design patterns. Now let us practice its use. Suppose you are to implement C++ classes for a sports orgnaization (say ATP, Association of Tennis Professionals). You are to design classes for players in this sports. The following lists the aspects you need to model.
* Each player has a (distinct) ranking, starting from 1 (highest).
* The key aspect related to a player is whether the player can compete in a tournament. A tournament is to be held in certain month of the year (we only model one year). We use 1, 2, 3 and so on to refer to January, Feburary, March and so on. The following rules decides this.
* There are two kinds of rules: POSITIVE (at what condition, a player can play) and NEGATIVE (at what condition, a player CANNOT play).
* [POSITIVE] A tournament has a minimum ranking requirement (must rank higher than a specific ranking). Any player rank higher than (or same as) this is qualified.
* [POSITIVE] A player can get wild-card to enter the tournament.
* [NEGATIVE] A player may be in injury and if so, cannot play in the tournament. The injury may occur in some period of time (say from January to March).
* [NEGATIVE] A player may be under disciplinary probation (within some period, starting and ending month) and cannot play in the tournament.
* How to determine qualification of a player? (i) If any of NEGATIVE rule says the players CANNOT play, then the player cannot play. (ii) If there is no rule preventing a player to play, then a player can play if there is a POSITIVE rule saying the player can play. How are you going to implmeent this? The most straightforward way is storing these information in the Player class. And then use if-then-else to decide whether a player can play. However, this is not ideal: why not? We will use the decorator pattern to work on this.